   idealised chain (an unlearned link is answered wrongly with certainty), Q = {last input}:
    L =   10: sum_t Pr[round t meets Q unasked] = 10.000 (<= L = 10); last mixture: max_n Pr * (n+1)/L = 1.32 at n = 98  (sqrt(L) = 3.2);  bound for round T holds
    L =   40: sum_t Pr[round t meets Q unasked] = 40.000 (<= L = 40); last mixture: max_n Pr * (n+1)/L = 2.55 at n = 1598  (sqrt(L) = 6.3);  bound for round T holds
    L =  160: sum_t Pr[round t meets Q unasked] = 160.000 (<= L = 160); last mixture: max_n Pr * (n+1)/L = 5.06 at n = 25598  (sqrt(L) = 12.6);  bound for round T holds
== F: Proposition 6.3 (batches), same scaffold and class as in A (human in the class)
    batches of 1 rounds, S = 4: sum_s E Lambda(M^[s]) = 0.839534 <= L ln(W/w(P)) = 8.499640   (holds)
    batches of 2 rounds, S = 2: sum_s E Lambda(M^[s]) = 0.409596 <= L ln(W/w(P)) = 8.499640   (holds)
    batches of 4 rounds, S = 1: sum_s E Lambda(M^[s]) = 0.194105 <= L ln(W/w(P)) = 8.499640   (holds)
   referee's remark: a scaffold whose inputs do not depend on the answers; all n questions
   are drawn and answered in ONE batch, and the mixture of a uniformly chosen prefix is deployed
    sum over the prefixes t = 1..5 of E Lambda(M of the first t-1 answers) = 1.248281 <= L ln(W/w(P)) = 5.991465   (holds)
== G: Proposition 1.15(c): law of the asked input when the human's answer continues the run
    max_x |Pr[x asked in the variant] - d_M(x)/L| = 1.39e-17
